[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-9-1-2",
    "slot_key": "Failover and recovery behavior",
    "slot_value": "Needs very clean handoff between operators or control stations; must account for degraded communications, lost-link recovery, missions where payload tasking changes while the aircraft is already underway, different ground stations or command setups, varying sensor loads, differing automation levels, and transitions between local crew, remote crew, or control elements. The mission commander should be able to pause the mission, override a specific operator action, reassign tasks, take full control for safety, communications loss, or a major mission deviation, and hand control back to the operator once the situation is resolved and the transfer is acknowledged. Anything indicating imminent loss of vehicle, loss of control, or a critical subsystem failure should trigger an automatic safeguard or mission pause even if nobody can respond right away. The controlling station may change, and authority needs to move cleanly with the aircraft so there’s no ambiguity about who can command it. The system should support intermittent links, delayed updates, and a clear state showing whether a station is local, remote, primary, or backup. Allowed actions may differ by setup, since some ground stations might only monitor while others can fully command, especially during coalition operations or when a platform is being supported from a reach-back site. A station is monitor-only when it doesn’t have the current authority, when the link is too limited for safe command, or when policy restricts it because of mission sensitivity, certification, or airspace constraints. It can command when it has the assigned control authority, the communications path is good enough, and the operator is cleared for that mission segment. That role can change during the mission, especially during handoff between sites or if the primary station loses connectivity and a backup has to take over. When new tasking arrives while the aircraft is already underway, the current mission plan should be preserved rather than overwritten, the new tasking should be shown as a change or re-tasking request, and the operator should decide whether it can be merged, queued, or whether the aircraft must transition to a new profile. For preplanned surveillance, keep the approved route and payload setup unless an operator explicitly authorizes a change. For time-sensitive retasks, support quick acceptance, impact assessment, and a clear indication of what in the current plan is being displaced. In degraded communications or lost-link recovery, handling should be more protective, with automatic safing and only limited changes allowed until control is re-established; payload changes underway should be possible only if the platform and payload can verify the change safely without interrupting flight. The handoff should make the new station state very explicit with a visible indication of who currently has authority, who is in standby, and whether the aircraft is in transition. It should lock out simultaneous command from multiple stations and require a clean acknowledgment from both outgoing and incoming controllers so there is no overlap. If the handoff is incomplete or the link is unstable, the system should hold the aircraft in a safe interim state and keep command with the last confirmed authority until the transfer is resolved. The active mission should be shown as owned by the current controlling station or unit, while new tasking should be shown as a pending proposal or external request until approved and applied, and queued requests should remain visibly separate from the live mission state until authorized. If the request is just informational or still under review, it should stay separate from the live mission. If it’s compatible with the current route, payload, timing, and authority, then it can be linked or merged so the operator can apply it without rebuilding the mission from scratch. If it’s related but not yet approved, I’d want it shown alongside the live mission as a candidate change so the controller can compare them without any risk of accidentally executing it. If a retask fits within the current authority and doesn’t conflict with a critical phase of flight or collection, the system should accept it quickly and immediately assess what it would displace. It should then show the operator the impact on timing, route, payload use, and any already approved tasking before anything is executed. If the displacement is significant or could create a safety or mission conflict, it should hold for review rather than auto-applying.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
